# std - the CT standard library, published as a normal ct-pkg package.
#
# The repository root is the package root; the `std/` directory under it is the
# namespace root; the consumer tool supplies the `-I <store>/ct/std/<version>`
# include root from ct.lock, so `import std::<mod>::<Unit>` resolves.
#
# Version policy: 0.x means the surface may break in a minor bump. See README.md.
#
# `requires` is measured, not guessed. The floor is 0.67.0:
#   - literal receivers (0.20.0) and CT type aliases (0.20.3) are historical;
#   - Vec<T> / Map<K,V> became the compiler's built-in layer in 0.30.0;
#   - cross-unit Drop needs 0.48.0 and own-able factory calls need 0.49.0;
#   - a .h.ct unit's own <base>.shim.h (auto-include + -I) is v0.64.0 (PH-65),
#     and every resource unit here uses one.
# The floor is 0.67.0 because that is the only version fully verified; 0.64-0.66
# are feature-compatible but untested. The resource-object protocol is
# documented in docs/design/decision-resource-ownership.md.
#
# -lm rides in `ldflags` because std::math calls sqrt / fabs / isnan / isinf;
# on platforms where libm is part of libc it is a no-op.
#
# `publisher` is the official package identity: ct 0.73+ uses it for the
# store path (ct/std) and lock rows. Older compilers (0.67) accept the key and
# skip unknown keys, so `ct build`/`tests/run.sh` are unaffected. The include
# root is the package root itself, hence `ns-root = .` (the `std/` segment of
# `import std::<mod>::<Unit>` comes from the directory tree, not this key).
name = std
publisher = ct
ns-root = .
version = 0.20.0
licence = MIT
requires = ct >= 0.67.0
ldflags = -lm
